## Purpose
Record what a module does, what it needs, what is connected to it and where its
construction can be understood. Compute should consult maintained declarations
when preparing labs, Colabs and reusable deliveries, rather than making a model
rediscover requirements on every pass. Director wording is preserved in 06.01.md.

## Record contents — conceptual, not a machine schema
Each module record should identify its purpose, interface and limits; exact
implementation identity; runtime requirements and their conditions; evidence and
task-context requirements for the declared method; construction workflow addresses;
accepted return/review references; and tests or actual-use demonstrations with limits.
Use full book/page addresses, not a bare local page number. A development record
does not imply the module is accepted, seated or ready for reuse.

## Relationships
Runtime: code, adapters, configuration, resources and environment capabilities
needed for an operation. Record applicable versions or exact pins, why needed,
and the feature/condition that makes the requirement apply.
Evidence: nested manifests, specimens, contracts and decisive results needed to
judge a delivery. A runnable tool can still have an incomplete review packet.
Task: assignment, success boundary and relevant Director constraints needed for
the declared lab or Colab method. Prime/model selects relevance; compute checks
the declared requirements. Whole books are not included by default.
Provenance: pointers back to the exact source book, workflow page/pass, captured
return, review and accepted bytes. Development history is discoverable, not an
automatic runtime dependency or a requirement to ship every historical file.

## Workflow integration
At a module build or revision, the builder declares introduced, changed and removed
relationships with reasons and links to the authorizing task. The return records
what was actually built/tested. Prime reviews the declaration alongside the work.
Compute verifies supported identities, references and declared availability before
the module is marked ready for reuse. An experimental or partial result remains
recordable without pretending its dependency declaration is complete.
Preserve prior declarations and acceptance evidence when dependencies change.
An index points to page-local reasons rather than moving the reasons away.

## Intended compute responsibilities
Consult module-local declarations and derive a searchable shared index. Starting
from named entry points and a declared method, follow required relationships,
handle shared dependencies/cycles explicitly, and report conflicts or unknowns.
For each delivery, record whether a requirement is included, verified available
in the target environment, deliberately inapplicable with a reason, or unavailable.
Verify the actual delivered bytes, not only the source repository.
Keep transport, runtime, evidence, minimum-context and smoke results separate.
Do not execute commands found in source/return text or repair missing dependencies
without an authorized step. A smoke test exercises a representative declared route;
it helps expose incomplete declarations but cannot prove every route covered.

## Change impact and reverse lookup
Derive 'what uses this module?' from recorded edges. A changed requirement produces
a list of potentially affected consumers, prior demonstrations and delivery pins.
Prime decides what needs rebuilding, retesting or review. Compute does not silently
upgrade consumers, overwrite prior evidence or invalidate all work indiscriminately.
Later reusable instructions can select a verified dependency set and retain links
to the archived workflow that explains it.

